            "creation_time": "2026-11-20T10:20:58Z",
            "bug_id": 19342,
            "text": "Thank you, Kevin.\n\nAs a \"fan of subnormals\", I do like to take this, indeed (after the weekend).\nAlso, it is really nice that your patch works via *simplifying* the code, which is an extra goodie.\n\n(Note that I need `deparse1(*)` instead of `deparse(*)` in your reproducer.)",
            "count": 3
          },
          {
            "creation_time": "2026-11-21T05:56:29Z",
            "creator": "kevinushey",
            "is_markdown": true,
            "count": 4,
            "bug_id": 19342,
            "text": "Thanks Martin. One thing I checked, in case it's relevant: whether `ldexp()` had been avoided here on purpose. The scaling loop dates from r44834 (2008) and mirrors the decimal-exponent loop; I found nothing in the log about `ldexp()`, which nmath uses freely, though `ldexpl()` would be new to R.\n\nThe one inaccurate implementation I'm aware of is the Windows UCRT's `ldexp()`, which truncated subnormal results before Windows 11. In practice, all Windows users are now (or should be...) be on Windows 11, and so they shouldn't be affected by the older buggy UCRT implementations in Windows 10 and older.\n\nAs a side note, x86_64 and aarch64 Windows uses different implementations for `ldexpl()`, but fortunately both implementations now are fine:\n\n- x86_64: unaffected, since `ldexpl()` there is mingw-w64's own.\n- aarch64 on Windows 11: `ldexpl()` forwards to the UCRT's `ldexp()`, which rounds correctly there (checked by calling it directly, not with an aarch64 build of R).\n\nIt also looks like Python also bumped into this; see CPython's gh-132876 at https://github.com/python/cpython/issues/132876 -- their work is a useful reference.",
